# To-do lists, reminders and household tasks

A native to-do item is editable data, not a navigation step in a GOV.UK task list. Keep adding, completing, deleting and dismissing a reminder distinct. The person needs to know whether a change was saved and what remains outstanding.

## Scope and native ownership

Home Assistant represents each to-do list as an entity, with its state reflecting incomplete-item count. Integrations vary in supported item fields and operations. Native to-do cards own item persistence; these specifications and local examples do not add a task backend. [Official Home Assistant source](https://www.home-assistant.io/integrations/todo/), reviewed 11 November 2026.

This page completes the design specification for **HA-GAP-026**. Its examples are synthetic documentation, not a claim that a new native feature or adapter has been installed. The application integration must meet the task-specific acceptance below.

## States and content decisions

| Source condition | Required presentation | Boundary |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Needs action | Show item text and due context if supplied. | Do not invent a due time for a date-only item. |
| Completion requested | Keep a pending result separate from saved completion. | A checked visual control alone is not backend confirmation. |
| Completed | Retain a route to supported completed-item operations. | Completion is not deletion or dismissal of every related reminder. |
| Sync failed / uncertain | Retain the item and factual save outcome. | Do not duplicate it automatically on reconnect. |
| Empty list / empty filter | Describe whether no tasks exist or no tasks match. | A failed fetch must not say All done. |

## Integration contract

1. Read supported item fields and operations from the integration. Preserve item identifiers, title, status and due-date type; do not assume all lists accept descriptions, recurrence or due times.
2. Keep configuration of a reminder separate from list completion. A saved reminder rule is not evidence that a notification was delivered or read.
3. Use the native item/persistence path and account scope. Prevent unintended duplicate submission while an add/update is pending; reconcile the actual returned item before retrying.
4. For deletion or clearing completed items, name the count and scope before confirmation. Do not promise undo unless the implementation supplies it.

## Accessible interaction

- A completion control needs the full item name and current status. Keep keyboard focus meaningful when a completed item moves into another section.
- Provide textual due dates and overdue state; do not depend on a coloured dot. Explain time zone for timed reminders.
- Keep long task descriptions, notes and error messages readable at narrow widths; avoid placing interactive links inside another interactive row.

## Failure and recovery

- If a list becomes unavailable, retain only clearly labelled last-known items and stop claiming its count is current.
- If adding returns an unknown outcome, inspect the current list before repeating; an identical title is not proof of identical item identity.
- For partial clear/delete operations, enumerate the remaining items and failed scope rather than saying the list was cleared.

